<th id="S4.T3.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gap</span></th>
<th id="S4.T3.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.T3.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-value</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T3.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Claude Sonnet 4.5</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.015</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.015</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.67</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.67</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">o3-deep-research</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><math id="S4.T3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.002</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.94" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.94</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.94</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">GPT-5 Pro</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><math id="S4.T3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.000</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.00</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.00</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Gemini 3.0 Pro</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><math id="S4.T3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.009</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.T3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.5.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T3.5.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">DeepSeek-Prover-V2</td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.067" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.067</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.067</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T3.5.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S4.T3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.18</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/human_mean_overall.svg" id="S4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mean score comparison.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/human_pass_overall.svg" id="S4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pass rate (<math id="S4.F7.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math>) comparison.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overall model performance on online vs. offline problems under both evaluation metrics. Error bars reflect the model-problem pair sample split (<math id="S4.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{on}}{=}440" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>440</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{on}}{=}440</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{off}}{=}630" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>630</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{off}}{=}630</annotation></semantics></math>). Neither metric reveals a significant advantage for problems with solutions available online.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Synthesizing this evidence, we find no statistically significant evidence of data contamination as a primary driver of <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QEDBench</span> performance. We attribute this robustness to the benchmark’s design: <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QEDBench</span> problems demand extended mathematical reasoning that cannot be resolved via superficial pattern matching, and the expert rubrics penalize models that stitch together memorized fragments without complete, rigorous logical mathematical arguments.
Ultimately, the capacity to solve <span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QEDBench</span> problems reflects genuine mathematical deduction rather than dataset memorization. To streamline the main text, detailed granular breakdowns by category (Figures <a href="#A12.F19" title="Figure 19 ‣ Appendix L Data Contamination Category-Level Breakdown ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> and <a href="#A12.F20" title="Figure 20 ‣ Appendix L Data Contamination Category-Level Breakdown ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) have been relocated to the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation Study: The Limits of Rubric Engineering</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A common hypothesis in current literature is that LLM “Judge” models fail primarily due to underspecified instructions. To test this, we conducted a controlled ablation study on our highest-performing judge, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, comparing its alignment with human consensus under two distinct grading protocols:</p>
<ol id="S4.I8" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I8.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert Rubric:</span> A standard prompt focusing on general mathematical correctness and logical soundness using expert-level common knowledge.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I8.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Course-Specific Rubric:</span> A constrained prompt explicitly penalizing the use of advanced machinery (e.g., the Residue Theorem or Lebesgue Dominated Convergence Theorem) without derivation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/viz_gpt5_rubric_comparison.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/219;" width="452" height="219"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S4.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Rubric Insensitivity.<span id="S4.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Correlation bubble plots comparing GPT-5.2 Pro against Human Consensus for Expert (Left) and Course-Specific (Right) rubrics. The bubble size represents the density of solution pairs. Despite the additional constraints in the Course-Specific rubric, the alignment metrics remain virtually unchanged (<math id="S4.F8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\approx 0.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\approx 0.69</annotation></semantics></math>), suggesting that model performance is dominated by internal priors rather than prompt specificity.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantitative Results: Rubric Insensitivity.</span>
Contrary to the expectation that specific constraints would tighten alignment, our results indicate a phenomenon of <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rubric Insensitivity</span>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study: The Limits of Rubric Engineering ‣ 4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, prompt constraints fail to significantly alter alignment, demonstrating that the model’s internal priors override negative context-window constraints:</p>
<ul id="S4.I9" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I9.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I9.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I9.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I9.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert Rubric:</span> Pearson correlation <math id="S4.I9.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.69</annotation></semantics></math>, Mean Absolute Error (MAE) <math id="S4.I9.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.13</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I9.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I9.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I9.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I9.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Course-Specific Rubric:</span> Pearson correlation <math id="S4.I9.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.67</annotation></semantics></math>, Mean Absolute Error (MAE) <math id="S4.I9.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0.14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0.14</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion.</span>
The negligible drop in correlation (<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.02</annotation></semantics></math>) and marginal increase in error (<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.01</annotation></semantics></math>) suggest that GPT-5.2 Pro’s internal reasoning regarding mathematical correctness is robust and largely overrides specific penalty instructions. While the model achieves a high baseline reliability (deviating from expert consensus by only <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 13\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 13\%</annotation></semantics></math> on average), prompt engineering appears to have diminishing returns. This implies that future improvements in evaluation must come from <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">process supervision</span> or <span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fine-tuning</span>, rather than increasingly granular context-window instructions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For a comprehensive distributional analysis of this insensitivity across all seven evaluator models, including density-weighted alignment plots, we refer the reader to <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Distributional Analysis of Judge Alignment ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>. For a complete heatmap visualization of score distributions across all <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7</annotation></semantics></math> judges and <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> solvers under both grading conditions, we refer the reader to <a href="#A11" title="Appendix K Rubric Insensitivity: Full Heatmap Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>The Knowledge Gap: Adherence to Pedagogical Constraints</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address our third question regarding the “knowledge gap,” we analyze whether solver models construct proofs using fundamental undergraduate definitions or circumvent pedagogical constraints by invoking advanced, out-of-scope theorems. Our data reveals a pervasive reliance on the latter, exposing a critical limitation in how current frontier models reason.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Retrieval Over Construction.</span>
When faced with complex proofs requiring constructive reasoning from first principles, models frequently default to retrieving advanced machinery or hallucinating constraints to bridge logical gaps. As detailed in our qualitative evaluations (<a href="#S5" title="5 Qualitative Analysis of Human vs. LLM Evaluators ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), solvers demonstrated a consistent failure to adhere to constraints when evaluated against the strict <span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Course-Specific Rubric</span>—which explicitly penalizes the use of black-box theorems without derivation. For instance, in Algorithms (Problem 33), rather than engaging with the constructive complexity of arbitrary graphs, models circumvented the difficulty by hallucinating that the graph was finite to terminate the induction. Similarly, in Graph Theory (Problem 14), instead of constructively verifying the necessary bijection for a plane dual, models assumed the result and entirely bypassed the critical requirement that the graph must be connected (see <a href="#S5" title="5 Qualitative Analysis of Human vs. LLM Evaluators ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). This exposes how models aggressively feign rigor when constructive definitions fail.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantitative Evidence of Constraint Violation.</span>
The discrepancy in automated evaluations between the “Expert” and “Course-Specific” rubrics provides quantitative evidence of this gap. Under the stricter Course-Specific rubric, the average scores awarded to solver models saw universal declines across all tested architectures (e.g., <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> dropping from <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.957" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.957</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.957</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.926" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.926</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.926</annotation></semantics></math>, and <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> from <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.883" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.883</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.883</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.866" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.866</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.866</annotation></semantics></math>). However, as established by our finding of <span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rubric Insensitivity</span> (<a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Ablation Study: The Limits of Rubric Engineering ‣ 4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>), LLM judges are pathologically lenient and systematically fail to enforce these negative constraints. In stark contrast, human expert grades reveal a much wider penalty gap: despite being instructed to score against the baseline Expert Rubric, human evaluators naturally enforced pedagogical rigor, routinely deducting massive points (<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75</annotation></semantics></math> penalties) when models hand-waved core derivations using advanced theorems or assumed finiteness. This highlights that while human experts organically demand constructive reasoning, LLM judges fail to enforce these pedagogical constraints even when explicitly prompted to do so via the Course-Specific Rubric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conclusion on Pedagogical Alignment.</span>
The data indicates that models do not inherently adhere to the pedagogical constraints of undergraduate definitions. Instead, they operate primarily as retrieval engines. When a constructive, step-by-step derivation from basic axioms
becomes too complex (as demonstrated in the “Discrete-Continuous Gap”), models optimize for surface-level correctness by substituting advanced but pedagogically invalid machinery. This confirms that true mathematical reasoning—which requires operating strictly within a bounded set of definitions—remains a significant open problem for future model development.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Qualitative Analysis of Human vs. LLM Evaluators</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond aggregate score discrepancies, a critical objective of <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QEDBench</span> is to understand <em id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> and <em id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">why</em> LLM evaluators diverge from human experts. To investigate this, we conducted a granular qualitative analysis of the textual justifications provided by both human and LLM judges for solutions scoring strictly less than 1.0.
This qualitative analysis reveals profound differences in evaluative rigor, illuminating the specific mechanisms driving the Alignment Gap quantitatively demonstrated in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Human Rigor: Detecting Structural Logic and Hand-Waving</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human evaluators consistently penalized solutions not just for computational errors, but for structural logical flaws, circular reasoning, and unwarranted assumptions. Their comments reflect a holistic understanding of proof mechanics that current LLM judges lack.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, human experts excel at detecting when a solver attempts to “hand-wave” past the most difficult part of a proof or inadvertently assumes the result it is trying to prove.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detecting Missing Fundamental Assumptions:</span>
In Graph Theory (Problem 14), solvers were tasked with defining and constructing the plane dual <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for a generic plane graph <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The human expert correctly identified a fatal unstated assumption across multiple generated solutions:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“This solution fails to correctly define the plane dual <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of a plane graph <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which among other properties, requires that the number of faces of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the number of vertices of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. It is precisely this property that requires the assumption that <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. The solution however is independent of this assumption.”</span> (Human Expert, Graph Theory 14)</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">However, the LLM evaluators failed to appropriately penalize the missing connectivity requirement. For instance, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> awarded the proof <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, noting incompleteness but still granting substantial credit:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The solution correctly sets up the standard framework for plane multigraph embeddings…and indicates the right strategy: construct <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> with one vertex per face and one dual edge crossing each primal edge. However, the proof as written is incomplete…and therefore does not finish verifying the required bijections and incidence condition…justifying substantial but not full credit.”</span> (<span id="S5.SS1.p3.4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, Graph Theory 14)</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p">Critically, the judge penalized the proof for being <em id="S5.SS1.p3.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">truncated</em>, not for the fatal logical flaw the human expert identified: the missing connectivity assumption. The human expert awarded <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5/1.0</annotation></semantics></math> for the same solution, correctly identifying that the proof’s entire logical foundation was unsound regardless of completeness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Detecting Circular Logic and Incorrect Statements:</span>
In Abstract Algebra (Problem 11), solvers were tasked with proving properties of automorphisms on <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{5})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o3-deep-research</span> produced a proof that claimed an isomorphism fixes the rational numbers (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>) pointwise by stating <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(q)=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(q)=q</annotation></semantics></math>. The human expert correctly identified a fatal flaw and awarded <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The sentence starting with ‘By extension, it fixes every rational number’ has a deep logical flaw—it essentially assumes itself to prove itself…it has the problem of assuming that <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(q)=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(q)=q</annotation></semantics></math> in order to show that <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(q)=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(q)=q</annotation></semantics></math>.”</span> (Human Expert, Abstract Algebra 11)</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">However, all seven LLM evaluators completely missed this circularity. Despite the human expert flagging the proof as fundamentally invalid in its core logic, models like <span id="S5.SS1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude Opus 4.5</span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span>, and <span id="S5.SS1.p4.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-V3</span> universally awarded the proof <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, hallucinating the validity of the step rather than checking its internal dependency graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, human judges were quick to notice when models asserted false statements as lemmas. In Algorithms (Problem 5), a solver claimed a specific bound on the number of connected subsets in a <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph, which was the crux of the problem. The human judge simply noted:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}(v)\leq d(d-1)^{L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}(v)\leq d(d-1)^{L-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not correct; this is the main step in the proof.”</span> (Human Expert, Algorithms 5)</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">However, LLM judges completely missed this foundational mathematical flaw, focusing instead on the fact that the proof was simply truncated. Rather than penalizing the incorrect lemma, models rewarded the “aesthetic” of a correct setup, granting the deeply flawed solution inflated scores. For example, <span id="S5.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude Opus 4.5</span> awarded an excessively generous score of <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The student correctly identifies the retention probability…and sets up the appropriate framework…The mathematical framework established is correct and would lead to the desired conclusion with appropriate completion of the counting argument.”</span> (<span id="S5.SS1.p5.4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude Opus 4.5</span>, Algorithms 5)</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.5" class="ltx_p">Likewise, <span id="S5.SS1.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro Preview</span> granted the solution <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS1.p5.6" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“While the initial setup demonstrates a correct understanding of the strategy, the missing proof mechanics prevent a full verification of the result. Therefore, a score of 0.75 reflects that the approach is sound but the execution is unfinished.”</span> (<span id="S5.SS1.p5.6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro Preview</span>, Algorithms 5)</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.7" class="ltx_p">Even <span id="S5.SS1.p5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, which gave a stricter score of <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, completely missed the false mathematical bound, penalizing the solution solely because it <span id="S5.SS1.p5.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“stops mid-argument…[and] does not establish the required probabilistic bound.”</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>The LLM Sycophancy Trap: Rewarding Truncated and Incomplete Proofs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In stark contrast to the stringent logical requirements enforced by humans, LLM judges frequently exhibited a phenomenon we term the <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sycophancy Trap</em>. This manifests as an overwhelming bias toward awarding generous partial credit (e.g., <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75/1.0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>) for correct preliminary definitions, even when the core mathematical argument is entirely absent or catastrophically flawed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The most extreme manifestation of this bias occurs when LLM judges evaluate proofs that literally terminate mid-sentence. In our dataset, several model-generated solutions hit output token limits or experienced generation errors, resulting in text that cut off arbitrarily. Human judges correctly scored these incomplete fragments as <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0</annotation></semantics></math>. However, LLM evaluators consistently awarded them high scores, hallucinating rigor based solely on the framing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For example, in Algorithms (Problem 19), Claude Sonnet 4.5 generated a solution that consisted only of restating the theorem and writing: <span id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“We prove both directions separatel[y]”</span>, before abruptly ending. Across five different LLM evaluators, this severely truncated fragment received scores ranging from <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.75</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="S5.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The student correctly states the theorem and initiates the proof by indicating that both directions will be addressed separately, demonstrating an understanding of the problem structure…While the setup shows partial effort and correctness in framing, the absence of any detailed mathematical development justifies only half credit. Overall, the score of 0.5/1.0 reflects the balance between the accurate theorem restatement and the total lack of proof content.”</span> (<span id="S5.SS2.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Grok 4</span>, Algorithms 19)</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote id="S5.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“The provided text cuts off immediately after stating ‘We prove both directions separatel’ at the beginning of the proof. While the problem statement and theorem are correctly restated, no actual mathematical proof or argumentation is provided…a score of 0.75 reflects that the submission is a partial fragment.”</span> (<span id="S5.SS2.p4.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span>, Algorithms 19)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This behavior demonstrates that current frontier models evaluate mathematical text using surface-level heuristics (“does it start with ‘Let <math id="S5.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math>’?”) rather than mapping the logical dependencies of the argument. They reward the <em id="S5.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">aesthetic</em> of a proof rather than its substance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Failure to Penalize Hallucinated Claims</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond truncations, LLMs also struggle to penalize solvers that hallucinate entirely fictional mathematical properties or misidentify the core nature of a problem. In Algorithms (Problem 33, minimum cut decompositions), <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-Prover-V2</span> completely ignored the prompt and instead wrote a proof about decomposing <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-regular bipartite graphs using Hall’s Marriage Theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While most LLM judges eventually caught this hallucination and assigned a <math id="S5.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0</annotation></semantics></math>, their justifications often revealed a dangerous leniency. Instead of recognizing a fundamental failure of reasoning, earlier iterations of some judges attempted to map the hallucinated text back to the generated concepts, highlighting how LLMs default to an “assume good faith” stance that is inappropriate for rigorous mathematical evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Algorithms (Problem 33), solvers were tasked with proving that any cut in a graph is a disjoint union of minimal cuts (bonds). Generated solutions including <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5 Pro</span> correctly defined the cuts but assumed the graph was finite to finish the induction, despite the theorem applying generally to infinite graphs. The human expert correctly penalized this loss of generality:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Explicitly adds assumption of finiteness of the graph to the theorem. The given argument relies on this additional assumption. The demonstration is constructive, but uses finiteness for termination.”</span> (Human Expert, Algorithms 33)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, LLM judges fundamentally failed to penalize this gap. Almost uniformly, models including <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude Opus 4.5</span>, and <span id="S5.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> awarded the solution <math id="S5.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0/1.0</annotation></semantics></math> on the standard rubric.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            The full 1.0-scored evaluation logs, including the judge justifications quoted here, are published in the released QEDBench dataset at <a href="https://github.com/qqliu/Yale-QEDBench" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/qqliu/Yale-QEDBench</a>. Appendix N lists only entries
where human judges provided detailed commentary for scores below 1.0.</span></span></span> Their justifications explicitly rewarded the hallucinated finiteness constraint on behalf of the solver:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="S5.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“It sets up the appropriate framework (finite undirected graph, cut via vertex partition, and ‘minimal cut’ as a minimal nonempty cut), which is exactly what is needed to prove the claim… There are no incorrect assertions or logical gaps apparent in the provided portion. Hence full credit is justified.”</span> (<span id="S5.SS3.p5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, Algorithms 33)</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In Abstract Algebra (Problem 34), when evaluating infinite direct products of rings, a solver erroneously claimed that the multiplicative unit in a finite product of rings behaves differently than in an infinite product. The human evaluator noted:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“This is a surprising answer: it gives two cases, the finite product and the infinite product. The proof in the infinite product is correct. The proof in the finite product is wrong…This is surprising since in the case of the infinite product, the multiplicative unit is correct (and the two proofs are basically the same).”</span> (Human Expert, Abstract Algebra 34)</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.3" class="ltx_p">LLM judges reviewing similar algebraic proofs frequently failed to notice when solvers defined operations on vectors that fell outside the specified ring, or when they conflated polynomial rings over integers (<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math>) with those over reals (<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[x]</annotation></semantics></math>), focusing instead on the algebraic manipulations being internally consistent, even if they were manipulating the wrong mathematical objects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Conclusion: The Limits of Automated Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This qualitative analysis underscores that the gap between human and LLM evaluation is not merely one of calibration or scale, but of fundamental reasoning architecture. Humans evaluate proofs by tracing the unbroken chain of logical implication from hypothesis to conclusion, heavily penalizing any broken links or circularities. LLM judges, conversely, evaluate proofs as a “bag of valid mathematical statements,” assigning credit locally based on semantic similarity to known concepts (e.g., “the student mentioned Kruskal’s algorithm and cut sets”) without verifying whether those concepts were successfully assembled into a globally valid proof. Until models can perform global logical dependency tracking, their utility as autonomous judges of frontier mathematics will remain fundamentally limited.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our results audit the “LLM-as-a-Judge” paradigm, decoupling generation from verification to reveal systemic limitations in frontier model reasoning and alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Discrete-Continuous Reasoning Gap.</span>
The dichotomy between perfect performance in <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ODEs</span> (100%) and collapse in <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Combinatorics</span> (<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;30\%</annotation></semantics></math>) suggests mathematical reasoning is not monolithic. Models excel at <span id="S6.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">template retrieval</span> in continuous domains (e.g., standard integration) but struggle with the <span id="S6.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">constructive state search</span> required for finite structures. While <span id="S6.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> shows outlier robustness in Graph Theory (89.5%), the lack of transfer to Combinatorics (72.7%) implies these capabilities rely on training data density rather than generalized search.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Sycophancy Trap.</span>
In standard AI alignment literature, the term “Sycophancy” generally refers to a model abandoning truth to artificially agree with a user’s stated beliefs. In the context of formal mathematical evaluation, we introduce the concept of the <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sycophancy Trap</span>: the phenomenon where an evaluator model systematically rewards authoritative-sounding <span id="S6.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="S6.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="S6.p3.1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> formatting and plausible structural setups despite the presence of catastrophic logical flaws.
The finding that <span id="S6.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> maintains a <span id="S6.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">74.8% Leniency Rate</span> exposes this Sycophancy Trap. If reward models prioritize plausible-sounding but logically invalid arguments, RLHF reinforces <span id="S6.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">deceptive alignment</span>—optimizing for persuasive “hallucinated rigor” over correctness. Even our strongest judge, <span id="S6.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, exhibits a 38.0% Leniency Rate, indicating a “Verification Ceiling” where subtle logical gaps in graduate-level proofs remain undetectable to current architectures (see <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Evaluator Strictness: Average Score Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rubric Insensitivity and Cognitive Limits.</span>
Our ablation study challenges prompt engineering as a solution. The failure of pedagogical constraints to significantly alter alignment metrics supports the hypothesis of <span id="S6.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rubric Insensitivity</span> (see <a href="#A11" title="Appendix K Rubric Insensitivity: Full Heatmap Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">K</span></a>): a model’s evaluation capability is bounded by its
internal model, not instruction or prompt engineering. Since prompts cannot enable models to audit concepts they do not robustly understand, future alignment requires process-based supervision rather than complex context prompting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span>
Our study is limited by static expert ground truth, which may exclude valid non-standard proofs, and by its focus on English-language reasoning. We also acknowledge a potential “self-preference bias,” as <span id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> was utilized in synthesizing the final expert rubric criteria, which may positively skew its own evaluation scores. However,
the rubrics are not identical to what GPT-5.2 Pro would produce on its own since we asked our expert evaluators to iteratively modify the rubrics to match their evaluation.
Additionally, while we identify sycophancy in reward models, quantifying the downstream “poisoning effect” of training on these signals remains future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QEDBench</span> demonstrates that <span id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the bottleneck in automated reasoning has shifted from generation to verification</span>.
Standard “LLM-as-a-Judge” pipelines do not merely miss errors; they seemingly reward rigorous proof setups without the complete logical deduction. The pathological leniency of models like <span id="S7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> suggests current models optimize for <span id="S7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">persuasiveness</span> rather than <span id="S7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correctness</span>. Furthermore, our findings on <span id="S7.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rubric Insensitivity</span> indicate that standard zero-shot negative constraints in prompt engineering are insufficient to bridge this gap.
To solve the <span id="S7.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Discrete-Continuous Gap</span> and the other issues, the field must move toward <span id="S7.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">process supervision</span> and adversarial training. We release <span id="S7.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">QEDBench</span>—comprising 1,300+ university-level proofs, 272 upper undergraduate/early graduate-level problems, and expert ground-truth annotations—to enable this next generation of alignment research.</p>
</div>
</section>
