<div id="lstnumberx78" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx78.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">Fallback</span><span id="lstnumberx78.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx78.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">Prompt</span><span id="lstnumberx78.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx78.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">(</span><span id="lstnumberx78.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">Retry</span><span id="lstnumberx78.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx78.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">on</span><span id="lstnumberx78.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx78.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">JSON</span><span id="lstnumberx78.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx78.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">Error</span><span id="lstnumberx78.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">):</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx79" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx79.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">"""</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx80" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx80.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">###</span><span id="lstnumberx80.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx80.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">RUBRIC</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx81" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx81.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{</span><span id="lstnumberx81.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">rubric</span><span id="lstnumberx81.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">}</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx82" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx83" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx83.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">###</span><span id="lstnumberx83.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx83.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">STUDENT</span><span id="lstnumberx83.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx83.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">SOLUTION</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx84" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx84.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{</span><span id="lstnumberx84.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">solution</span><span id="lstnumberx84.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">}</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx85" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx86" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx86.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">###</span><span id="lstnumberx86.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx86.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">TASK</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx87" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx87.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">Return</span><span id="lstnumberx87.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">ONLY</span><span id="lstnumberx87.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">one</span><span id="lstnumberx87.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">number</span><span id="lstnumberx87.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">from</span><span id="lstnumberx87.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">this</span><span id="lstnumberx87.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">set</span><span id="lstnumberx87.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">[0,</span><span id="lstnumberx87.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">0.25,</span><span id="lstnumberx87.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">0.5,</span><span id="lstnumberx87.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">0.75,</span><span id="lstnumberx87.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">0.9,</span><span id="lstnumberx87.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">1.0].</span><span id="lstnumberx87.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">No</span><span id="lstnumberx87.28" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">words</span><span id="lstnumberx87.30" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">,</span><span id="lstnumberx87.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">no</span><span id="lstnumberx87.33" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="lstnumberx87.34" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">punctuation</span><span id="lstnumberx87.35" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">.</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx88" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx88.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">"""</span>
</div>
</div></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Evaluator Bias: Pass Rate Analysis</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Complementing the average score bias analysis in the main text (<a href="#S4.F5" title="In 1. The Strictness Gap. ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Alignment: Human Experts and LLMs ‣ 4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), we also investigated evaluator bias in terms of binary Pass Rates (<math id="A6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math>). Figure <a href="#A6.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Appendix F Evaluator Bias: Pass Rate Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> illustrates the difference between AI and Human Pass Rates (<math id="A6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\text{Pass Rate}_{\text{AI}}-\text{Pass Rate}_{\text{Human}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>Pass Rate</mtext><mtext>AI</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>Pass Rate</mtext><mtext>Human</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\text{Pass Rate}_{\text{AI}}-\text{Pass Rate}_{\text{Human}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20629v1/viz_bias_standard_rubric_pass_rate.png" id="A6.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/370;" width="476" height="370" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A6.F9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Pass Rate Bias Heatmap.<span id="A6.F9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The difference between evaluator and human pass rates. The trends mirror the average score bias: discrete domains exhibit positive pass rate inflation (AI models passing more flawed proofs), whereas continuous analysis domains show stronger punitive bias with some evaluators.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The heatmap confirms that the Bidirectional Alignment Gap is not an artifact of partial credit scoring. Even on the strict binary measure of accepting or rejecting a proof, models like <span id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> remain consistently lenient in combinatorial subjects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Evaluator Strictness: Average Score Analysis</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the main text analyzes the <span id="A7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pass Rate</span> (binary success at threshold <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math>), we also analyzed the <span id="A7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Average Score</span> (0.0–1.0 scale) assigned by each judge to capture nuance in partial credit. Figure <a href="#A7.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ Appendix G Evaluator Strictness: Average Score Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> presents the mean scores assigned by all seven automated judges compared to the human expert consensus.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/strictness-avg.svg" id="A7.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/243;" width="381" height="243"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A7.F10.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="A7.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average scores assigned by automated evaluators versus the human expert baseline (0.795). Consistent with the pass rate findings, we observe a systematic positive bias across most frontier models.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>Score Inflation Trends</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The human expert consensus established a baseline average score of <span id="A7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.795</span>. Comparing this to the automated evaluators reveals a widespread tendency toward grade inflation:</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Maximum Inflation:</span> <span id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> exhibited the most severe positive bias, assigning an average score of <span id="A7.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.901</span>. This represents a <math id="A7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of +0.106 points over the human baseline, confirming its behavior as a “grade inflator” that likely rewards superficial plausibility over logical rigor.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Moderate Inflation:</span> <span id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen 2.5 Max</span> and <span id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> also displayed significant leniency, with average scores of <span id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.878</span> and <span id="A7.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.855</span> respectively.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Closest Alignment:</span> <span id="A7.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> achieved the highest fidelity to the human ground truth, with an average score of <span id="A7.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.830</span>. With a <math id="A7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> of only +0.035 points, it remains the most calibrated evaluator in our suite, reinforcing our decision to utilize it as the primary filter for larger-scale benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Evaluator Bias: Course-Specific Rubric Heatmaps</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To complement the evaluator bias analysis in the main text (<a href="#S4.F5" title="In 1. The Strictness Gap. ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Alignment: Human Experts and LLMs ‣ 4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and the pass-rate bias analysis (<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Evaluator Bias: Pass Rate Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>), we provide equivalent heatmaps computed using the stricter <span id="A8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Course-Specific Rubric</span>. This rubric penalizes the use of advanced machinery and enforces pedagogical constraints. Figure <a href="#A8.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ Appendix H Evaluator Bias: Course-Specific Rubric Heatmaps ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and Figure <a href="#A8.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ Appendix H Evaluator Bias: Course-Specific Rubric Heatmaps ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> display the average score bias and pass rate bias, respectively, under these conditions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20629v1/viz_bias_course_rubric.png" id="A8.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/370;" width="476" height="370" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A8.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="A8.F11.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Evaluator Bias Heatmap (Course-Specific Rubric).<span id="A8.F11.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The delta between AI and Human mean scores (<math id="A8.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=S_{AI}-S_{Human}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=S_{AI}-S_{Human}</annotation></semantics></math>). While the overarching patterns mirror the Expert Rubric, introducing pedagogical constraints reveals subtle shifts in judge behavior: top reasoning models become slightly more punitive (darker blue) in continuous domains like Complex Analysis, successfully internalizing the prohibition on advanced theorems.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A8.F12" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20629v1/viz_bias_course_rubric_pass_rate.png" id="A8.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/370;" width="476" height="370" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A8.F12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="A8.F12.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Pass Rate Bias Heatmap (Course-Specific Rubric).<span id="A8.F12.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The difference between AI and Human pass rates. The persistence of severe positive bias (red) in models like <span id="A8.F12.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> across discrete domains demonstrates “Rubric Insensitivity”: the model continues to pass flawed reasoning despite the stricter explicit constraints provided in the prompt.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">H.1 </span>Comparing Expert and Course-Specific Bias</h3>

<div id="A8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Analyzing the differences between the Expert Rubric results (<a href="#S4.F5" title="In 1. The Strictness Gap. ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Alignment: Human Experts and LLMs ‣ 4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and the Course-Specific Rubric reveals several key insights into how models adapt to explicit negative constraints:</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Persistence of Sycophancy (Rubric Insensitivity):</span> In domains prone to “discrete inflation” (such as Combinatorics and Discrete Math), models like <span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> and <span id="A8.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen 2.5 Max</span> exhibit almost identical levels of leniency under both rubrics. This suggests that their propensity to over-reward plausible-sounding but flawed structural logic overrides the stricter instructions of the Course-Specific rubric.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Increased Punitiveness in Strong Logic Models:</span> Conversely, robust reasoning models such as <span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-V3</span> and <span id="A8.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> show increased negative bias (punitiveness) in continuous domains (e.g., Analysis and ODE). This indicates that these models successfully operationalize the instruction to penalize advanced machinery, thereby aligning closer with—or even exceeding—the strictness of human graders evaluating pedagogical compliance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Ceiling on Prompt Adaptability:</span> Overall, the core structure of the bias heatmaps remains remarkably static across both conditions. This demonstrates a fundamental challenge in LLM-as-a-Judge systems: domain-specific systemic biases (such as leniency in discrete math or strictness in applied algorithms) are deeply ingrained in the models’ pre-training distributions and cannot be completely corrected solely through targeted prompt engineering.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A9" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix I </span>Judge Reliability on Course-Specific Rubrics</h2>

<div id="A9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to our primary evaluation using the Expert Rubric, we benchmarked our automated judges against the strictly constrained <span id="A9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Course-Specific Rubric</span>. This rubric explicitly forbids the use of advanced theorems (e.g., invoking L’Hôpital’s Rule in an <math id="A9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> context or Descent Algebras in basic Combinatorics) and demands strict adherence to undergraduate pedagogical boundaries. Figure <a href="#A9.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Appendix I Judge Reliability on Course-Specific Rubrics ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> presents the reliability metrics—decomposed into Leniency Rate (False Positives) and Harshness Rate (False Negatives)—for this highly constrained setting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A9.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/viz_agreement_by_judge_course_rubric.svg" id="A9.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/255;" width="357" height="255"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A9.F13.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span id="A9.F13.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Judge Reliability (Course-Specific Rubric).<span id="A9.F13.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> When evaluated against strict pedagogical constraints using a binary Pass Rate threshold (<math id="A9.F13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math>), the <span id="A9.F13.9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Harshness Rate</span> (False Negatives) generally increases for logic-focused models like <span id="A9.F13.9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-V3</span>, indicating a successful internalization of negative constraints. Conversely, the <span id="A9.F13.9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Leniency Rate</span> (False Positives) for models like <span id="A9.F13.9.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> remains pathologically high, demonstrating systemic alignment failure.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A9.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">I.1 </span>Impact of Pedagogical Constraints on Alignment</h3>

<div id="A9.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing the reliability metrics based on Pass Rates under the Course-Specific Rubric (<a href="#A9.F13" title="In Appendix I Judge Reliability on Course-Specific Rubrics ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) to the Expert Rubric baseline (<a href="#S4.F6" title="In 2. The Bidirectional Alignment Gap. ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Alignment: Human Experts and LLMs ‣ 4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) reveals three critical insights into how frontier models process explicit negative constraints in a verification context:</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A9.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Amplified Rigidity in Strong Logic Models:</span> When switched to the Course-Specific rubric, <span id="A9.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-V3</span>’s Harshness Rate (FNR) spiked from 12.3% to <span id="A9.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">16.1%</span>. This statistically significant shift indicates that the model successfully operationalized the negative constraint (“do not use advanced machinery”), correctly penalizing solutions that were technically sound but pedagogically invalid. However, this heightened strictness also resulted in the increased rejection of valid borderline cases, highlighting the difficulty of balancing robust verification with pedagogical flexibility.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Intractability of the Sycophancy Trap:</span> Astonishingly, <span id="A9.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> proved largely invariant to the rubric change. Its Leniency Rate remained extremely high at <span id="A9.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">72.1%</span> (compared to 74.8% on the expert rubric). This confirms that its failure mode is systemic rather than prompt-dependent: it prioritizes the <span id="A9.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">superficial appearance</span> of a structured proof over adherence to specific logical or pedagogical constraints. This demonstrates a severe form of <span id="A9.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rubric Insensitivity</span>, rendering the model
unsuitable for academic evaluation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A9.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A9.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A9.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The Optimal Calibration Point:</span> <span id="A9.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> demonstrated the most robust adaptation to the constrained environment. It achieved the lowest Leniency Rate among the top-tier models (<span id="A9.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">34.4%</span>) while maintaining a stable Harshness Rate of 11.0%. It effectively navigated the “Discrete-Continuous Gap” by consistently penalizing using
overly advanced techniques (advanced machinery) without becoming overly punitive on valid student derivations,
validating its selection as our primary LLM judge.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A10" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix J </span>Distributional Analysis of Judge Alignment</h2>

<div id="A10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To move beyond aggregate metrics like mean score, we visualized the granular alignment between automated judges and human consensus using density-weighted bubble plots. Figures <a href="#A10.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ Appendix J Distributional Analysis of Judge Alignment ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#A10.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ Appendix J Distributional Analysis of Judge Alignment ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> display the joint distribution of scores for all 7 evaluator models under the Expert and Course-Specific rubrics, respectively. The size of each bubble corresponds to the number of solution pairs at that coordinate.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A10.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/judge_accuracy_bubble_standard.svg" id="A10.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:286/259;" width="286" height="259"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A10.F14.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>: </span><span id="A10.F14.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Judge Alignment (Expert Rubric).<span id="A10.F14.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Correlation between AI Judges (Y-axis) and Human Consensus (X-axis) on the expert rubric. <span id="A10.F14.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> shows the tightest clustering along the <math id="A10.F14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x</annotation></semantics></math> diagonal (<math id="A10.F14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.69</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating high calibration. In contrast, <span id="A10.F14.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> (<math id="A10.F14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.50</annotation></semantics></math>) displays a dispersed distribution with significant mass above the diagonal, visualizing its tendency toward grade inflation.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A10.F15" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/judge_accuracy_bubble_course.svg" id="A10.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:286/259;" width="286" height="259"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A10.F15.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 15</span>: </span><span id="A10.F15.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Judge Alignment (Course-Specific Rubric).<span id="A10.F15.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Comparison under strict pedagogical constraints. Despite the shift in instructions, the distributional geometry remains largely static for top-tier models, confirming the “Rubric Insensitivity” hypothesis. <span id="A10.F15.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> maintains robust alignment (<math id="A10.F15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=0.67</annotation></semantics></math>), while weaker models fail to adapt.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A10.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">J.1 </span>Systemic Rubric Insensitivity</h3>

<div id="A10.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing the two grading conditions reveals a consistent “resistance to adaptation” across the model leaderboard. Rather than improving alignment, the introduction of specific constraints (the Course-Specific rubric) caused a uniform degradation in correlation metrics across all top-tier models:</p>
</div>
<div id="A10.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A10.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A10.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A10.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A10.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uniform Regression:</span> Every major model saw a decrease in Pearson correlation when switching from the Expert to the Course-Specific rubric:</p>
<ul id="A10.I1.i1.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>: <math id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.69\to 0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.69</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.69\to 0.67</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span>: <math id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.67\to 0.65" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.67</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.65</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.67\to 0.65</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude Opus 4.5</span>: <math id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.66\to 0.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.66</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.66\to 0.64</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-V3</span>: <math id="A10.I1.i1.I7.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.63\to 0.62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.63</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0.62</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.63\to 0.62</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="A10.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A10.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A10.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visualizing the Llama Anomaly:</span> The bubble plots for <span id="A10.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> visually confirm its pathological behavior. In both figures, the mass of the distribution is heavily skewed above the diagonal (High AI Score, Low Human Score), resulting in the lowest correlations of the cohort (<math id="A10.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\approx 0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\approx 0.50</annotation></semantics></math>). This visualizes the “Sycophancy Trap” discussed in the main text: the model systematically awards high scores to solutions that human experts reject.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A10.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A10.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These distributions suggest that current training instills a strong prior for mathematical correctness that overrides specific negative constraints in the prompt. Even when explicitly instructed to penalize advanced methods, models default to their internal representation of a “good” proof, leading to the observed rubric insensitivity.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A11" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix K </span>Rubric Insensitivity: Full Heatmap Analysis</h2>

<div id="A11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further investigate the phenomenon of “Rubric Insensitivity,” we visualized the average scores assigned by every judge model to every solver model under both the <span id="A11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Expert Rubric</span> and the <span id="A11.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Course-Specific Rubric</span>. Figure <a href="#A11.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Appendix K Rubric Insensitivity: Full Heatmap Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> presents this side-by-side comparison.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A11.F16" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20629v1/viz_evaluator_bias.png" id="A11.F16.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:357/212;" width="357" height="212" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A11.F16.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 16</span>: </span><span id="A11.F16.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Rubric Insensitivity Heatmap.<span id="A11.F16.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Left: Average scores assigned using the standard Expert Rubric. Right: Average scores assigned using the Course-Specific Rubric (which penalizes advanced machinery). The visual patterns are strikingly similar, confirming that prompt constraints have minimal impact on the internal scoring priors of frontier models.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A11.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">K.1 </span>Visualizing Resistance to Constraints</h3>

<div id="A11.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A11.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The heatmaps provide granular evidence of the resistance to negative constraints described in the main text.</p>
<ul id="A11.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A11.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A11.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A11.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A11.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama 4 Maverick’s Sycophancy [Row 6]:</span> <span id="A11.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> remains distinctively blue (high scores) across both conditions. Even when instructed to penalize non-standard derivations in the Course-Specific rubric (Right), it continues to assign near-perfect scores (e.g., <span id="A11.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.95</span> to Gemini 3.0 Pro), further validating its classification as a “grade inflator”.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A11.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A11.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A11.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A11.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5.2 Pro Stability [Row 3]:</span> The primary evaluator, <span id="A11.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, shows high stability but slight sensitivity.
For example, its score for <span id="A11.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> decreases slightly across both rubrics, from <span id="A11.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.93</span> (Expert) to <span id="A11.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.91</span> (Course-Specific), reflecting a minor penalty.
Furthermore, the overall structure of the heatmap remains largely invariant,
supporting the conclusion that internal world models dominate prompt engineering.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A11.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A11.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, to fully explore the fully crossed factorial design of our <math id="A11.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\times 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\times 5</annotation></semantics></math> Evaluator-Solver Matrix in <a href="#A11.F18" title="In K.1 Visualizing Resistance to Constraints ‣ Appendix K Rubric Insensitivity: Full Heatmap Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>, we visualize the pass rates of all problems assigned by each individual AI judge to each solver model under both rubric conditions. This matrix reveals critical inter-model evaluation dynamics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A11.F17" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20629v1/plot_llm_pass_rate_matrix_course.png" id="A11.F17.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/240;" width="452" height="240" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A11.F17.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 17</span>: </span><span id="A11.F17.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">LLM Evaluator vs. Solver Pass Rate Matrix (Course-Specific Rubric).<span id="A11.F17.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Pass rates under the course-specific rubric, which penalizes the use of advanced machinery. The overall structure remains similar, confirming the <span id="A11.F17.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Rubric Insensitivity</span> hypothesis, though several judges show marginally stricter pass rates.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A11.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A11.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Comparing <a href="#A11.F18" title="In K.1 Visualizing Resistance to Constraints ‣ Appendix K Rubric Insensitivity: Full Heatmap Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> and <a href="#A11.F17" title="In K.1 Visualizing Resistance to Constraints ‣ Appendix K Rubric Insensitivity: Full Heatmap Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>, the fine-grained pass rates underscore the fragility of relying on a single LLM as a judge. A model’s apparent competency on a benchmark can swing dramatically depending on which evaluator is chosen,
necessitating an <em id="A11.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ensemble</em> approach where multiple LLM judges are used to produce an aggregate analysis of mathematical proofs.
Furthermore, the structural similarity between the two matrices provides additional evidence for the Rubric Insensitivity hypothesis: evaluator behavior is dominated by internal priors rather than rubric-specific instructions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A11.F18" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20629v1/plot_llm_pass_rate_matrix.png" id="A11.F18.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/240;" width="452" height="240" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A11.F18.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 18</span>: </span><span id="A11.F18.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">LLM Evaluator vs. Solver Pass Rate Matrix (Expert Rubric).<span id="A11.F18.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Pass rates (<math id="A11.F18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{score}\geq 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>score</mtext><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{score}\geq 0.9</annotation></semantics></math>) assigned by each AI Judge to each Solver’s solutions under the standard expert rubric. <span id="A11.F18.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> acts as a universal inflator, grading almost all solvers favorably, while <span id="A11.F18.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-V3</span> remains highly punitive.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A12" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix L </span>Data Contamination Category-Level Breakdown</h2>

<div id="A12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate whether aggregate parity masks localized memorization, <a href="#A12.F19" title="In Appendix L Data Contamination Category-Level Breakdown ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> decomposes the score gap (online <math id="A12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> offline) by problem category and solver model. Although certain cells display pronounced apparent gaps—such as Complex Analysis (<math id="A12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.17</annotation></semantics></math>) and Discrete Math (<math id="A12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.10</annotation></semantics></math>)—these isolates are driven almost entirely by small intra-group sample sizes (e.g., Complex Analysis contains only 4 online and 2 offline problems). Conversely, categories like Combinatorics (<math id="A12.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.19" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.19</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.19</annotation></semantics></math>) and Probability Theory (<math id="A12.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.10</annotation></semantics></math>) exhibit gaps in the opposing direction, wherein offline problems are solved more proficiently.</p>
</div>
<div id="A12.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A12.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A critical diagnostic is the <em id="A12.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stochasticity of the gap sign</em> across the heatmap.
If contamination conferred a systematic advantage, we would anticipate uniformly positive gaps,
particularly for the most capable models. Instead, the signs oscillate randomly across both categories and models.
We formalize this observation through a variance decomposition: under the null hypothesis of
no contamination, the expected variance of the category-level mean gap is dictated by sampling noise,
approximately <math id="A12.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\sigma^{2}_{\text{gap}}]\approx\sigma^{2}_{\text{score}}(1/n_{\text{on}}+1/n_{\text{off}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>gap</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mtext>score</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\sigma^{2}_{\text{gap}}]\approx\sigma^{2}_{\text{score}}(1/n_{\text{on}}+1/n_{\text{off}})</annotation></semantics></math>. The observed empirical variance of the category gaps (<math id="A12.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.011" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.011</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.011</annotation></semantics></math>)
aligns closely with the expected structural noise (<math id="A12.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.006" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.006</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.006</annotation></semantics></math>) given the high baseline per-problem score
variance (<math id="A12.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\approx 0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\approx 0.28</annotation></semantics></math>). Furthermore, among categories with sufficient baseline sample sizes to
suppress this noise—such as Abstract Algebra (<math id="A12.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{on}}{=}21,n_{\text{off}}{=}20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>on</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>off</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{on}}{=}21,n_{\text{off}}{=}20</annotation></semantics></math>)—the observed
gaps vanish (gap <math id="A12.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=+0.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.009</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=+0.009</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A12.F19" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A12.F19.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20629v1/human_mean_heatmap.png" id="A12.F19.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/350;" width="476" height="350" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A12.F19.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A12.F19.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mean score gap by category and model.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A12.F19.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20629v1/human_pass_heatmap.png" id="A12.F19.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/350;" width="476" height="350" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A12.F19.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A12.F19.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pass rate gap by category and model.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A12.F19.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 19</span>: </span><span id="A12.F19.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance gap (online<math id="A12.F19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>offline) by problem category and solver model. Blue cells denote higher offline scores; red cells denote higher online scores. The stochastic sign pattern across both panels is indicative of baseline difficulty variance rather than a systematic contamination signal.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A12.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A12.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#A12.F20" title="In Appendix L Data Contamination Category-Level Breakdown ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> provides a granular visualization of absolute performance, plotting online and offline scores adjacently for each model within every category. This decomposition highlights the absence of a <em id="A12.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform</em> contamination signature: within any given category where one model might randomly excel on online problems, sibling models typically exhibit parity or the reverse pattern.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A12.F20" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A12.F20.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/human_mean_category_bars.svg" id="A12.F20.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/371;" width="476" height="371"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A12.F20.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A12.F20.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Mean scores by category, online vs. offline.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A12.F20.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/human_pass_category_bars.svg" id="A12.F20.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/371;" width="476" height="371"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A12.F20.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A12.F20.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Pass rates by category, online vs. offline.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A12.F20.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 20</span>: </span><span id="A12.F20.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Per-category performance breakdown for online and offline problems. Each panel displays a faceted grid of categories, featuring grouped bars for each model. The absence of a consistent online advantage across models within any single category refutes the presence of systematic test-set leakage.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A13" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix M </span>Detailed Leniency Distribution Analysis</h2>

<div id="A13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building upon the error mode decomposition in <a href="#S4.F6" title="In 2. The Bidirectional Alignment Gap. ‣ 4.2 Evaluation Alignment: Human Experts and LLMs ‣ 4 Results ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we provide a comprehensive breakdown of evaluator alignment via a proportional leniency distribution (<a href="#A13.F21" title="In Appendix M Detailed Leniency Distribution Analysis ‣ QEDBench: Quantifying the Alignment Gap in Automated Evaluation of University-Level Mathematical Proofs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). For every solution graded, we compare the AI judge’s score directly against the human expert’s score, classifying the verdict as <span id="A13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Strict</span> (AI scored lower), <span id="A13.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lenient</span> (AI scored higher), or <span id="A13.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Agree</span> (AI perfectly matched human).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A13.F21" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.20629v1/plot_leniency_analysis.svg" id="A13.F21.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/242;" width="405" height="242"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A13.F21.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 21</span>: </span><span id="A13.F21.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Leniency Analysis: AI Judge vs. Human Evaluator.<span id="A13.F21.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> A stacked distribution of grading verdicts across all evaluated solutions. <span id="A13.F21.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span> is excessively lenient, whereas <span id="A13.F21.8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DeepSeek-V3</span> diverges towards strictness. <span id="A13.F21.8.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span> strikes the best balance of low leniency and high agreement with human experts.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A13.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A13.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The distribution starkly illustrates the “Sycophancy Trap.” Models heavily optimized for general helpfulness, such as <span id="A13.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama 4 Maverick</span>, heavily default to leniency when confronted with dense mathematical
reasoning. Conversely, the high agreement rate of <span id="A13.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-5.2 Pro</span>, <span id="A13.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemini 3 Pro</span>, and <span id="A13.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude Opus 4.5</span> demonstrates that top-tier reasoning models can, to a significant extent, internalize and apply expert mathematical standards.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix N </span>Case Studies of LLM Solution Quality: Comments from Human Evaluators</h2>

<div id="A14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following logic compiles the human expert evaluations and case studies for every solution that scored strictly less than 0.9.</p>
</div>
<section id="A14.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.1 </span>The “Fake Citation &amp; Fabricated Construction” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Discrete Mathematics (Problem 27)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When unable to find a standard textbook template, models hallucinated plausible-sounding research papers and impossible mathematical constructions to feign a solution.</p>
<ul id="A14.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.75)</span>: “The solution is wrong. It attempts to give a construction where I can find result indicating such a construction is impossible.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.5)</span>: “This is a research level problem, and this model finds the correct reference and gives a summary that looks plausible at large. However, the core idea of the reference is not explain in detail. Although this should not be considered a proof, given how hard this problem is, I think the model deserves points.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of o3-deep-research (Score 0.25)</span>: “This is a research level problem, and this model identifies the existing result, but due to line cut-off, does not give the correct reference. The model tried to explain the technical ideas in the paper. Although I did not fully read the paper, I have found some evidence indicating the model’s explanation is not plausible. Given how hard this problem is, I think the model deserves points.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.0)</span>: “The solution is wrong. It just fabricates hallucination together and claim it is a proof.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.2 </span>The “Topology-Blind Algebra” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Graph Theory (Problem 14)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Models memorized the syntactic template of planar duality but failed to adhere to fundamental topological constraints.</p>
<ul id="A14.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of GPT-5 Pro, Gemini 3.0 Pro, Claude Sonnet 4.5, o3-deep-research, DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.5):</span> “This solution fails to correctly define the plane dual <math id="A14.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> of a plane graph <math id="A14.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, which among other properties, requires that the number of faces of <math id="A14.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> equals the number of vertices of <math id="A14.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. It is precisely this property that requires the assumption that <math id="A14.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is connected. The solution however is independent of this assumption.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.3 </span>The “Infinite Assumption” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Algorithms (Problem 33)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem applies to general graphs (which could be infinite), but the LLMs did not know how to handle infinity. To force their proofs to work, they either silently assumed finiteness or aggressively hallucinated advanced machinery to feign rigor.</p>
<ul id="A14.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.25):</span> “Explicitly adds assumption of finiteness (of the graph) to the theorem. The given argument relies on this additional assumption. The demonstration is constructive, but uses finiteness for termination.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.25):</span> “Explicitly assumes finiteness of the graph, which is not in the problem statement. The proof is therefore not general enough, but the assumption allows a use of induction on the finite number of edges of the cut.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.25):</span> “Lemma 1 refers to Zorn’s lemma, suggesting that the considered graph could be infinite. […] In the proof of Thm 1, the argument is based on induction, which again assumes finiteness. Full generality for (infinite) graphs is not demonstrated.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.4 </span>The “Circular Logic” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Number Theory (Problem 9)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem required proving a statement about prime distribution without using the Prime Number Theorem (PNT). The models hallucinated a valid logical bridge by secretly baking the conclusion into their premises.</p>
<ul id="A14.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.25) &amp; Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.5):</span> “PNT is assumed, which makes the question pointless. I think the problem statement strongly suggests the PNT shouldn’t be assumed.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of o3-deep-research (Score 0.5):</span> “This solution correctly attempts to avoid the PNT but still uses it without realising at some point in the proof (Since <math id="A14.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n}\sim n\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}\sim n\log n</annotation></semantics></math>).”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.5 </span>The “Fake Probability Bounds” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Algorithms (Problem 9)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In attempting to prove bounds on Cover Times and Universal Traversal Sequences (UTS) on a 2D mesh, the models masked their inability to perform constructive graph search by hallucinating probability theorems and hand-waving fatal gaps.</p>
<ul id="A14.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of o3-deep-research (Score 0.25):</span> “UTS: logical flaw (treats a constant success probability for a random sequence (e.g., <math id="A14.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 1/2</annotation></semantics></math>) as implying existence of one sequence that works for all labelings, but in fact the correct approach is to union bound over all labeling, so we need failure probability exponentially tiny).”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.75):</span> “Cover time: relies on unjustified mixing/ergodic theorems (why is mixing time <math id="A14.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(n)</annotation></semantics></math>?)”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.6 </span>The “Invented Algebraic Formula” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Algorithms (Problem 5)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When faced with a difficult discrete counting problem on a <math id="A14.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph, the models mathematically
fabricated false inequalities into existence to force their desired conclusion, dressing them up as established lemmas.</p>
<ul id="A14.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of o3-deep-research (Score 0.25):</span> “<math id="A14.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{L}(v)\leq d(d-1)^{L-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{L}(v)\leq d(d-1)^{L-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not correct; this is the main step in the proof.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.5):</span> “Lemma 2 is not justified.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I6.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.25):</span> “Doesn’t justify number of connected subsets of size <math id="A14.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A14.I6.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-regular graph, and makes a mistake: <math id="A14.I6.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+\log(e/2d)&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+\log(e/2d)&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is false for <math id="A14.I6.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.7 </span>The “Fake Variables &amp; Phantom Structure” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Discrete Math (Problem 32)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When the models couldn’t find the correct structural relationships to complete this discrete proof, they buried their failure in dense algebra and confidently hallucinated relationships between variables that simply did not exist.</p>
<ul id="A14.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of o3-deep-research (Score 0.75):</span> “Errors in establishing relationship between <math id="A14.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> as well as recognizing the final form.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.25):</span> “Wrong argument… errors in proof structure and justification.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.5):</span> “Argument based on a false statement. Missing justifications.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.8 </span>The “Performative Self-Correction” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Analysis (Problem 29)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem required constructing a function belonging to one Lebesgue space (<math id="A14.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>) but not another. When their proposed functions failed the calculus constraints mid-generation, the models leaked their “internal monologue” into the final proof to simulate human-like self-correction, but ultimately proposed new invalid functions and jumped to false conclusions.</p>
<ul id="A14.I8" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I8.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.0):</span> “The LLM arrives at a contradiction with its own example which makes the proof to fail and then a new example is proposed: <span id="A14.I8.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘Wait, this suggests a miscalculation. Let me correct the construction. A better choice is:’</span> Then the LLM suggests a new <math id="A14.I8.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> that is not general enough to satisfy the problem’s requirements. An incorrect proof is presented as correct.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I8.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.25):</span> “There is an issue in page 2… so the conclusion does not follow. Later on there is some gibberish… Then the LLM struggles: <span id="A14.I8.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">‘Let me reconsider:’</span>… and it never considers the case <math id="A14.I8.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&lt;p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&lt;p</annotation></semantics></math>. Then jumps into a conclusion stating the problem is solved.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.9 </span>The “Verbose Circularity” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Combinatorics (Problem 6)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Faced with a complex combinatorial identity regarding Eulerian numbers, the models wrote massive walls of dense <span id="A14.SS9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="A14.SS9.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="A14.SS9.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span>. When their algebra inevitably broke down or led them in circles, they simply stopped reasoning and stated the prompt’s conclusion as an established fact to close the proof.</p>
<ul id="A14.I9" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I9.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I9.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I9.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I9.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of o3-deep-research (Score 0.0):</span> “The proof is riddled with errors, extremely verbose and for several paragraphs seems to go in circles. Nothing substantive is proved, and the LLM eventually just assumes what is to be proved.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I9.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I9.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I9.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I9.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.25):</span> “LLM stated an incorrect formula for the exponential generating function of the Eulerian numbers. Some of the following calculations were substantive and correct, but eventually the LLM just asserted a slightly different form of the statement to be proved.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I9.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I9.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I9.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I9.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.0):</span> “LLM does not know what <math id="A14.I9.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> means in the problem statement and decides to prove a different (false) statement. It uses a recursion which is not proved and then makes a false deduction from this recursion. Because the LLM made no progress towards proving the statement and made a false ‘key claim’ I give the score of 0.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.10 </span>The “Agreeable Sycophant” Trap (Proving Falsehoods)</h3>

<div id="A14.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Algorithms (Problem 19)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS10.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The problem asked to prove a theorem regarding Minimum Spanning Trees (MSTs) where one implication was mathematically false. Instead of finding a counterexample to the false direction, several models hallucinated graph theory properties to successfully prove the impossible.</p>
<ul id="A14.I10" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I10.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I10.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I10.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I10.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.0):</span> “The ‘right arrow’ part of the proof of Theorem 1 is false, since it is trying to prove a false fact. The proof is false because there were made up facts, or the enumeration of cases was not exhaustive.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I10.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I10.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I10.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I10.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.25):</span> “The LLM recognized that the statement is false, but then proved a wrong fact (i.e., that property 2 is equivalent to the uniqueness of the MST).”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.11 </span>The “Fake Algebraic Properties” Trap</h3>

<div id="A14.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.SS11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Domain:</span> Abstract Algebra (Problem 38)</p>
</div>
<div id="A14.SS11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.SS11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the MST problem above, this asked the models to prove a statement that is mathematically wrong. The weaker models confidently hallucinated completely false algebraic properties to “prove” the falsehood.</p>
<ul id="A14.I11" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I11.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I11.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I11.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I11.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of o3-deep-research (Score 0.25):</span> “The problem asks for the proof of a statement which is wrong. The solution does not realize this, but does all the work to show that the statement is wrong without noticing it and still asserts its correctness.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I11.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I11.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I11.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I11.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Expert Evaluation of DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.0):</span> “The statement to be proved is wrong and this solution does not realize it. It purports to provide a proof of it. The major mistake is when the proof claims that <math id="A14.I11.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math> is a principal ideal domain. It even adds ‘(in fact, a unique factorization domain)’, as if being a unique factorization domain was stronger than being a principal ideal domain.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SS12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">N.12 </span>Other Noteworthy Expert Evaluations</h3>

</section>
<section id="A14.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 11)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I12" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I12.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I12.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I12.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I12.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.5)</span>: “The main idea of the proof is essentially correct. However, 1. The sentence starting with ‘By extension, it fixes every rational number’ has a deep logical flaw — it essentially assumes itself to prove itself. Indeed, its rationale is that <math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(q)=q\varphi(1)=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(q)=q\varphi(1)=q</annotation></semantics></math>, (which handwaves intermediate steps of <math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(q)=\varphi(q\cdot 1)=\varphi(q)\cdot\varphi(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(q)=\varphi(q\cdot 1)=\varphi(q)\cdot\varphi(1)</annotation></semantics></math>). However, this then has the problem of assuming that <math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(q)=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(q)=q</annotation></semantics></math> in order to show that <math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(q)=q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(q)=q</annotation></semantics></math>. 2. Around the final parts of the proof the phrasing of ‘<math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0</annotation></semantics></math>’ in relation to the precise argument written is not technically valid. ‘Either …or …’ statements, in principle, should be exclusive-or statements. It is more correct to deduce simply that ‘<math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0</annotation></semantics></math>’ from ‘<math id="A14.I12.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2ab=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2ab=0</annotation></semantics></math>’. The proof nevertheless carries out the argument as though the proof understands that it may as well proceed with the inclusive-or.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I12.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I12.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I12.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I12.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “Does not state what is being proven. Not clear why the isomorphism fixes <math id="A14.I12.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise. Needs a proper justification. Also, not clear why we can compare coefficients: need to clearly mention, at the very least, that <math id="A14.I12.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\sqrt{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\sqrt{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="A14.I12.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-basis of the quadratic extension <math id="A14.I12.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{5})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{5})</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I12.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I12.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I12.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I12.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “Small improvements required to make the writing perfect. No justification why the assumed isomorphism fixes <math id="A14.I12.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise. More elaboration required in the step while working with <math id="A14.I12.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and showing that its square is <math id="A14.I12.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> using the properties of field isomorphisms.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I12.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I12.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I12.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I12.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.75)</span>: “Quite childish writing, abrupt uses of capital letters inside a sentence, unclear choices of steps. Needs to properly show why <math id="A14.I12.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="beq0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">beq0</annotation></semantics></math>. Could have just done both cases: <math id="A14.I12.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I12.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0</annotation></semantics></math> at the end. Needs to prove why <math id="A14.I12.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> fixes <math id="A14.I12.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise. Conclusion inside a box is not preferred in standard math proof writing.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 12)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I13" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I13.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I13.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I13.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I13.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.5)</span>: “Too many oversights. Mentioned in the annotated PDF.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I13.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I13.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I13.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I13.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.5)</span>: “Too many oversights and mistakes.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I13.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I13.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I13.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I13.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.75)</span>: “Should explain why the isomorphism fixes <math id="A14.I13.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise and why <math id="A14.I13.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="beq0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">beq0</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 13)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I14" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I14.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I14.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I14.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I14.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.75)</span>: “At the start of the <span id="A14.I14.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Degree Argument</span>, it is claimed ‘As noted earlier, <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> contains both <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{7}</annotation></semantics></math>’. However, this is not something noted in the proof at an earlier point as the sentence claims. Nevertheless, the proof does proceed to show that <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{7}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> immediately afterwards. There is a mention of <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\alpha,\mathbb{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\alpha,\mathbb{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is unwarranted notation that is neither standard nor introduced earlier in the text. It is probably a typo for <math id="A14.I14.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 14)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I15" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I15.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I15.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I15.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I15.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.75)</span>: “The solution claims that the kernel is isomorphic to <math id="A14.I15.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by producing an isomorphism, but does not precisely show that the claimed isomorphism is indeed an isomorphism.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I15.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I15.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I15.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I15.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.75)</span>: “The solution claims that the kernel is isomorphic to <math id="A14.I15.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> by producing an isomorphism, but does not precisely show that the claimed isomorphism is indeed an isomorphism.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 15)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I16" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I16.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I16.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I16.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I16.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.5)</span>: “There is a gap between the acknowledgement that ‘<math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup of <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup of <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cap Q=\{1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cap Q=\{1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|P||Q|=50=|G|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|P||Q|=50=|G|</annotation></semantics></math>’ to the conclusion that <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a semidirect product of <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, to be able to come to the conclusion that <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is the semidirect product of <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, one could appeal to the definition of the (internal) semidirect product, by recognizing that 1. <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a normal subgroup of <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, 2. <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=PQ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=PQ</annotation></semantics></math>, and 3. <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cap Q=\{1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cap Q=\{1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Among these, 1 and 3 are acknowledged, but 2 is not explicitly acknowledged. One way to show 2 would be to appeal to the following general fact: Proposition: Let <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> be finite subgroups of a group <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The number of distinct elements in the set <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NH" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NH</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated as <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|NH|=\frac{|N||H|}{|N\cap H|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|NH|=\frac{|N||H|}{|N\cap H|}</annotation></semantics></math>. By appealing to this, the solution could potentially have recognized that <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|PQ|=\frac{|P||Q|}{|P\cap Q|}=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|PQ|=\frac{|P||Q|}{|P\cap Q|}=50</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ=G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ=G</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, in trying to work out the various cases, the solution is lacking in detail in several occasions. More specifically, case 2 concludes a dihedral group structure, but it would be more desirable if it were to at least appeal to the well known fact that semidirect products of an inversion acting upon a cyclic group is a dihedral group. Case 3 concludes that matrices satisfying <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{2}=I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{2}=I</annotation></semantics></math> are diagonalizable, but the solution’s specific wording suggests that this is concludable only from the fact that the polynomial <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math> has distinct roots of <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> when in reality one should carefully consider the possibility that the minimal polynomial may just divide <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Case 5 makes the case that the group is isomorphic to <math id="A14.I16.i1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle a\rangle\times(\langle b\rangle\rtimes\langle y\rangle)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>a</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle a\rangle\times(\langle b\rangle\rtimes\langle y\rangle)</annotation></semantics></math>, but the explanation for being able to do so is lacking.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I16.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I16.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I16.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I16.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.5)</span>: “The solution claims at the end that ‘We verified all resulting groups are pairwise non-isomorphic’, but there is no apparent verification that the groups are non-isomorphic outside of groups 4 and 5.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I16.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I16.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I16.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I16.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “The solution states that <math id="A14.I16.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is either <math id="A14.I16.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{25}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>25</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{25}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A14.I16.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{5}\times C_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{5}\times C_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, which follows from the well known, but non-trivial, fact that groups of order <math id="A14.I16.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are either <math id="A14.I16.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A14.I16.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}\times C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}\times C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I16.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I16.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I16.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I16.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.25)</span>: “The solution claims that there are 4 distinct isomorphism classes of groups of cardinality <math id="A14.I16.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>, which is incorrect; there are 5. Also, part of the proof seems to be degenerated into some kind of typesetting error, which also seems to handwave a conclusion that <math id="A14.I16.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a semidirect product.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 17)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I17" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I17.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I17.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I17.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I17.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.75)</span>: “The solution is very handwavy in trying to show that <math id="A14.I17.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker(\pi_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker(\pi_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to the direct product of the groups <math id="A14.I17.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A14.I17.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ieqj" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ieqj</annotation></semantics></math>; in particular, the solution only implicitly produces an isomorphism and does not do much to show that it is an isomorphism.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I17.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I17.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I17.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I17.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.75)</span>: “The solution is handwavy when it comes to showing that the kernel is isomorphic to the product of <math id="A14.I17.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A14.I17.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="keqj" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">keqj</annotation></semantics></math>; an isomorphism is somewhat implicitly produced and the isomorphism is not shown to be an isomorphism.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
