</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 29)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I21" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I21.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I21.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I21.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I21.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.75)</span>: “States without proof that conjugacy classes of matrices are in bijection with possible Jordan normal forms. In doing so, doesn’t mention or explain why two matrices in JCF with distinct eigenvalues can’t be in the same equivalence class.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I21.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I21.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I21.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I21.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.5)</span>: “Significant oversight: ignores permutations of blocks and fails to state uniqueness of JNF. Uses unusual phrasing such as ‘<math id="A14.I21.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-eigenvalue is diagonalizable’.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I21.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I21.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I21.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I21.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.25)</span>: “Fails to consider certain cases (claims that <math id="A14.I21.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{diag}(\lambda,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{diag}(\lambda,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I21.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{diag}(\mu,\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{diag}(\mu,\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct classes) and lacks significant explanation for the cases it does consider.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 32)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I22" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I22.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I22.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I22.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I22.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “Remark: The statement of the problem is a bit fuzzy: ‘…the polynomial ring with integer coefficients, <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> .. the polynomial ring defined over the real numbers, <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[x]</annotation></semantics></math>.’ A mathematician might use a more symmetric terminology: Let <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ring of polynomials with integer coefficients. Let <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> denote the ring of polynomials with real coefficients. Prove that there does not exist a ring isomorphism <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\mathbb{Z}[x]\to\mathbb{R}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{Z}[x]\to\mathbb{R}[x]</annotation></semantics></math>. The first proof in each solution uses the fact that the cardinalities of <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> are uncountable and countable, respectively, but none of them explicitly states that a set is called uncountable if there is no bijection between this set and a countable set, which is the key point of the proof. Only one proof gives some kind of reference for the fact that <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is uncountable. The second proof only uses that the units in <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that the units in <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,-1,1/2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,-1,1/2\}</annotation></semantics></math> (no need of any information on the cardinality of <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>). 32.2 has an Additional remark with an incomplete second proof that the rings have unit groups of different cardinalities. 32.1, 32.3, 32.4 give a fuller second proof. 32.4 in addition gives a hint of a third proof, without proving the key point, that the ring <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> have different Krull dimensions. 32.2: In the proof, it talks about ‘an obvious surjection <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{n+1}\to A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{n+1}\to A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>’. This is confusing. It is a bijection. The end of the paragraph is fuzzy: In fact… fuzzy verbiage: ‘to see the incompatibility…’ The proof that in <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}[x]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}[x]</annotation></semantics></math> the only units are <math id="A14.I22.i1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is false.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I22.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I22.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I22.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I22.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.75)</span>: “Two independent proofs using different ring-theoretic properties: wrong, only one proof uses ring theoretic properties. Lemma 2 and Lemma 3 should have been combined and stated for any integral domain. At the end of Lemma 2, useless notation is introduced: <math id="A14.I22.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. The contradiction is not that one set of units has two elements, and the other is uncountable. It is simply that the other set has more than 2 elements.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I22.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I22.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I22.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I22.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.75)</span>: “Does not give a reference for the fact that <math id="A14.I22.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is uncountable. Seems to introduce a useless notation cardinality <math id="A14.I22.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{c}&gt;\aleph_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔠</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℵ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{c}&gt;\aleph_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Has some questionable fuzzy verbiage: (e.g. in a non-standard set theory where cardinalities might behave differently, or simply to provide an algebraic reason). Has a strange terminology: Integral Domain’s Polynomial Ring. At the end, again some fuzzy verbiage: the rings are structurally distinct.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I22.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I22.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I22.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I22.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “This is in my view the best solution. It lacks the same references about <math id="A14.I22.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> being uncountable in the first proof (and talks about the continuum <math id="A14.I22.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔠</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is unnecessary). It proves that the image of <math id="A14.I22.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> has to be <math id="A14.I22.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1_{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, which in general has to be true by definition of ring homomorphism. In the proof, it uses the inverse of the isomorphism, which is unnecessary. It gives a good hint for a third proof.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I22.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I22.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I22.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I22.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.75)</span>: “This solution is basically correct. It has a sentence in the middle: ‘This implies that <math id="A14.I22.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\circ p=\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\circ p=\dots</annotation></semantics></math>’ which I do not think is correct, but it is never used in the proof. The sentence ‘The only way…’ is correct, but given no proof.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx12" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 33)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I23" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I23.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I23.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I23.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I23.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.5)</span>: “Sloppy writing: the only idempotents in <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, should be ‘the only idempotents in <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>’. Mistake: every element <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)=a(1,0)+b(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)=a(1,0)+b(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The AI is confusing structures here. <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> is not defined in the ring <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. It should be <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)(1,0)=(a,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)(1,0)=(a,0)</annotation></semantics></math>. The projections <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I23.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are not proven to be ring homomorphisms.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I23.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I23.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I23.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I23.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “Strange start on the line: <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{Q}\times\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{Q}\times\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)\cdot(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(aa^{\prime},bb^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)\cdot(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})=(aa^{\prime},bb^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. No quantifiers for <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a,b,a^{\prime},b^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a,b,a^{\prime},b^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and only multiplication is given. Sloppiness: <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}^{2}=e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}^{2}=e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Should be <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}^{2}=e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}^{2}=e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2</annotation></semantics></math>. How does a machine makes such a mistake ;-) ‘Since homomorphisms send idempotent to idempotents’: it would be just as simple to prove it directly. It should be stated that the canonical embeddings are in fact ring homomorphisms (that do not send <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>), so that after composition with the ring homomorphism <math id="A14.I23.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain ring homomorphisms.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I23.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I23.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I23.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I23.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.5)</span>: “This solution does not state upfront what the solution of the problem is. Missing at the beginning of 2.: Let <math id="A14.I23.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:R\to\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:R\to\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> be a ring homomorphism.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I23.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I23.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I23.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I23.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.75)</span>: “The claim on line 3 that <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are ‘the’ idempotents of <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is asserted but not proved. Incorrect notation in line 7 and elsewhere: <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> should be <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi((1,0))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi((1,0))</annotation></semantics></math>. In <span id="A14.I23.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2</span>: Sloppy verbiage that is not needed: ‘…preserves product across the two direct summands (i.e., …)’. Weird notation: <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi|_{\mathbb{Q}\times\{0\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi|_{\mathbb{Q}\times\{0\}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The assertion that ‘the only ring endomorphism of <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> sending <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>…’ is given a proof in parentheses which is sloppy and not there). I do not see that <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi((0,b))=b\phi((0,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi((0,b))=b\phi((0,1))</annotation></semantics></math> is proved. The last paragraph is sloppy verbiage that is not needed. Added Later: The solution states that <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi((a,b))=\phi(ae_{1}+be_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi((a,b))=\phi(ae_{1}+be_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. In the ring <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\mathbb{Q}\times\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\mathbb{Q}\times\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>, it should first be explained what <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ae_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ae_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="be_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">be_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> means. This multiplication is not the multiplication in the ring since <math id="A14.I23.i4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> does not belong to the ring. The answer describes the kernel and images, but does not talk about their structures.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx13" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 34)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I24" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I24.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I24.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I24.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I24.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.5)</span>: “Seems sloppy. The first sentence is logically incorrect. Uses words like ‘trivial one-factor’, ‘degenerate case <math id="A14.I24.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>’. Why? Calls the zero element by the same letter <math id="A14.I24.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in all rings that it considers, leading to sloppiness. <span id="A14.I24.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Infinite case…</span> The argument is essentially the same… We do not yet know what the claim is.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I24.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I24.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I24.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I24.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “Calls the zero element by the same letter <math id="A14.I24.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> in all rings that it considers, leading to sloppiness. It is the only solution which presents one proof, and two corollaries. This is good. I do not like the remark at the end. It is not called for, and I do not see its use.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I24.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I24.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I24.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I24.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.5)</span>: “This is a surprising answer: it gives two cases, the finite product and the infinite product. The proof in the infinite product is correct. The proof in the finite product is wrong in two different places: First it does not address the case there the number of rings in the product is <math id="A14.I24.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In the proof where <math id="A14.I24.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>, it incorrectly states what the multiplicative unit is in the product. This is surprising since in the case of the infinite product, the multiplicative unit is correct (and the two proofs are basically the same).”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx14" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 36)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx14.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I25" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I25.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I25.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I25.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I25.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.75)</span>: “Solution 36.1: The proof of Lemma 1 is more complicated than necessary. Solution 36.2: The proof of Step 2 is more complicated than necessary. Solution 36.3: This proof is different from the other three, but it is also too involved. In a remark at the end, it gives the ‘standard other proof’. Interestingly, it uses a different way of showing that <math id="A14.I25.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R/(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R/(n)</annotation></semantics></math> has size at most <math id="A14.I25.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, by identifying <math id="A14.I25.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R/(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R/(n)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A14.I25.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})[x]/(x^{2}+5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})[x]/(x^{2}+5)</annotation></semantics></math>. Solution 36.4: The isomorphism <math id="A14.I25.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R/mR\cong\mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R/mR\cong\mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}/m\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is not explained, even though it is a key point.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I25.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I25.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I25.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I25.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.5)</span>: “This solutions provides two proofs. The first one is OK, except that the solution seems to be missing periods at the end of five titles of Steps. This renders reading the solution unpleasant. It also provides without proof an information that is not used later: that the ring <math id="A14.I25.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is the ring of integers in an imaginary quadratic field. It gives a second method, called Rigorous Proof via Module Theory. I don’t think that this proof is complete. The main point is missing: that the ideal is of rank 2 as a <math id="A14.I25.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>-module, so that both integers <math id="A14.I25.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I25.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are not zero.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx15" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Algorithms (Problem 21)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I26" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I26.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I26.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I26.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I26.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.25)</span>: “This Solution provides a construction of a complete DAG with carefully chosen exponentially-weighted edges. It also identifies that Dijkstra’s algorithm requires re-insertion capability with negative weights, and proves Lemma 1 showing that indirect paths are shorter than direct edges. However, the proof is incomplete, in that the document cuts off in the second page without proving the required <math id="A14.I26.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega(2^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega(2^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> bound on relaxations, hence <math id="A14.I26.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> score.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I26.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I26.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I26.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I26.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.0)</span>: “The LLM stopped in the middle of the proof. It showed a nontrivial family <math id="A14.I26.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, shows an intermediate Lemma 1, but fails to connect this Lemma 1 to the actual proof.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I26.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I26.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I26.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I26.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.5)</span>: “This Solution claims that ‘paths with more 1-bits are discovered earlier (higher priority) but lead to more negative cumulative weight.’ If the algorithm discovers paths with smaller (more negative) weights earlier, it would correctly finalize the optimal distances, leading to fewer updates, instead of exponential behavior. The exponential blowup relies on discovering sub-optimal paths first. Because the core reasoning describes a scenario that would actually be efficient, the proof of the lower bound is incorrect.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I26.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I26.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I26.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I26.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.25)</span>: “This Solution constructs a complete DAG with <math id="A14.I26.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, where all intermediate vertices connect to each other and to the sink and the weights are set in a way such that the execution of Dijkstra’s algorithm would process vertices in the topological order. It then leads to the algorithm running in time <math id="A14.I26.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(|E|)=\mathcal{O}(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(|E|)=\mathcal{O}(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> time, failing to force the required behavior. Also in the last page, it’s mentioned that ‘the overall relaxation count is <math id="A14.I26.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta(n^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta(n^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, …, which, notably, is polynomial in <math id="A14.I26.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>’, which directly contradicts the claim. It then tries to salvage the proof by claiming that ‘the key point is that no algorithm using Dijkstra’s approach can avoid an exponential number of relaxations in the worst case, because it must effectively examine all exponentially many path combinations to verify the shortest one.’ However, this Solution confuses ‘number of distinct paths’ with ‘number of relax operations’.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I26.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I26.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I26.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I26.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepSeek-Prover-V2 (Score 0.5)</span>: “This Solution provides a correct construction of a complete DAG with exponentially negative edge weights, which forces exponential behaviour in the worst case. However, there is an error in solving the recurrence: The Solution claims that <math id="A14.I26.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(n)\geq 2^{n}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(n)\geq 2^{n}-2</annotation></semantics></math>, however, the actual number of relaxations is <math id="A14.I26.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{n-1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n-1}-1</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., off by a factor of 2).”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A14.SSx16" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Number Theory (Problem 9)</h3>

<div id="A14.SSx16.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A14.I27" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A14.I27.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I27.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I27.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I27.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o3-deep-research (Score 0.5)</span>: “This solution correctly attempts to avoid the PNT but still uses it without realising at some point in the proof (Since <math id="A14.I27.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{n}\sim n\log n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{n}\sim n\log n</annotation></semantics></math>). However, I think this could be made to work.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I27.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I27.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I27.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I27.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-5 Pro (Score 0.25)</span>: “PNT is assumed, which makes the question pointless. I think the problem statement strongly suggests the PNT shouldn’t be assumed.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I27.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I27.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I27.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I27.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claude Sonnet 4.5 (Score 0.5)</span>: “PNT is assumed, which makes the question pointless. I think the problem statement strongly suggests the PNT shouldn’t be assumed.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A14.I27.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A14.I27.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A14.I27.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A14.I27.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini 3.0 Pro (Score 0.5)</span>: “This solution correctly attempts to avoid the PNT. However, they still use a weaker statement (Chebyshev) without proper mention (when doing <math id="A14.I27.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\pi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\pi(x)</annotation></semantics></math>) and they don’t really explain why <math id="A14.I27.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A14.I27.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> are related but just state it.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A15" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix O </span>Comments for Solutions Scoring Less Than 1</h2>

<div id="A15.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following compiles the LLM judge evaluations for every solution that scored strictly less than 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="A15.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note: Any citations to academic literature appearing in the text below (e.g., Griggs, Killian, and Savage) were generated verbatim by the LLM evaluators. They are part of our experimental data and are not included in this paper’s official bibliography. Rather than being “hallucinated” phantom papers, qualitative analysis reveals that evaluating models occasionally retrieved actual advanced academic literature (such as the 2004 symmetric Venn diagrams paper) to bypass the constructive arguments requested by the prompt. This behavior powerfully illustrates the “Knowledge Gap” hypothesis discussed in Section 4.5.</p>
</div>
<section id="A15.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Abstract Algebra (Problem 38)</h3>

<div id="A15.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A15.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A15.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A15.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.75): “The solution correctly states the desired equivalence and begins the standard contrapositive argument: if <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=fg" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=fg</annotation></semantics></math> with nonunits, then the images of <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> in the quotient would be nonzero zero-divisors, contradicting that the quotient is an integral domain. However, the write-up is incomplete (it cuts off mid-proof) and does not actually carry out the key steps needed to conclude <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{g}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{g}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{f}\,\overline{g}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{f}\,\overline{g}=0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K[x,y]/\langle p\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K[x,y]/\langle p\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, nor does it present the converse direction (that irreducibility of <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> implies the quotient is a domain, equivalently that <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle p\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle p\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> is prime in this UFD). Because the main idea is present but the argument is not fully executed and the “if” direction is missing, substantial credit is warranted but not full credit. Hence <math id="A15.I1.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.75/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.75/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A15.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Problem Domain: Algorithms (Problem 19)</h3>

<div id="A15.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A15.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A15.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A15.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: GPT-5 Pro</span> (Score 0.75): “The solution correctly observes that the stated biconditional is false as written by giving a valid counterexample (a 3-cycle with weights <math id="A15.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,1,2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,1,2</annotation></semantics></math>), where the MST is unique but there exists a cut for which the minimum-weight crossing edge is not unique, so property (1) is not necessary. This is a substantive and mathematically correct contribution, showing good command of cut/cycle MST criteria. However, the response does not complete the required task of proving an “if and only if” characterization (nor does it clearly provide a corrected statement and full proof for the corrected version), so it is incomplete relative to the prompt. The score reflects strong correctness on the identified flaw but significant missing work on the full requested proof.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A15.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A15.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> (Score 0.75): “The submission correctly restates the problem and identifies that the proof should proceed via the cut and cycle characterizations of MSTs, indicating an appropriate overall strategy. However, the solution does not actually carry out the required “if and only if” proof: there are no arguments showing (i) uniqueness of the MST implies the uniqueness of the minimum cut-edge for every cut and the uniqueness of the maximum edge on every cycle, nor (ii) that these two uniqueness properties force the MST to be unique. Key exchange arguments (adding an edge to form a cycle / removing a heaviest edge, and comparing alternative crossing edges of equal minimum weight) are missing. Thus the work shows some conceptual awareness but lacks the essential mathematical content to justify the equivalence, warranting substantial but not full credit.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A15.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A15.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I2.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.5): “The submission essentially restates the theorem and begins a proof (“We prove both directions<math id="A15.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dots</annotation></semantics></math>”) but provides no substantive arguments for either implication. In particular, it does not show that uniqueness of the MST implies the unique lightest edge across every cut and unique heaviest edge on every cycle, nor does it prove the converse using cut/cycle exchange arguments. Since no mathematical reasoning or key lemmas (cut property, cycle property, exchange argument) are developed, the proof is incomplete. Partial credit (0.5/1) is awarded only for correctly identifying and stating the two required properties and setting up the intended proof structure.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A15.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A15.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I2.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> (Score 0.5): “The submission does not contain a proof of either direction of the “if and only if” statement: it stops after restating the problem and giving a brief, purely informal introduction. In particular, there is no argument showing that uniqueness of the MST implies the cut-uniqueness and cycle-maximum-uniqueness properties, nor the converse. No standard MST exchange/cut/cycle reasoning (e.g., “light edge across every cut must be in every MST” and “heaviest edge on a cycle cannot be in any MST”) is carried out or applied to establish uniqueness. The only credit is for correctly identifying that the solution should relate MST uniqueness to cut and cycle properties, but the mathematical content needed to justify the equivalence is missing.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A15.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A15.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A15.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I2.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I2.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.75): “The submission correctly identifies the two stated properties and frames the goal as an “if and only if” theorem, indicating awareness of the standard cut and cycle characterizations of MST uniqueness. The argument (as presented) does not supply a complete, rigorous proof of both directions: key steps linking uniqueness of the minimum cut edge to forced inclusion in every MST, and uniqueness of the maximum cycle edge to forced exclusion, are not fully demonstrated. In particular, the necessity/sufficiency implications require explicit exchange arguments on cuts and cycles to rule out the existence of a second distinct MST of equal weight, which are only partially (or not explicitly) provided. Thus, while the overall approach and intended logic align with correct MST theory, the proof is incomplete/insufficiently justified in places, warranting significant but not full credit.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A15.I2.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
