<p id="A15.I6.i21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I6.i21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: grok-4-0709 (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I6.i21.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.5): “The student’s solution correctly identifies the need to construct a measurable function on a subset of <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and introduces a parameter <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\in(0,1/(2p))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\in(0,1/(2p))</annotation></semantics></math>, which suggests an attempt to control integrability near zero or infinity, a common strategy for such problems. However, the definition of <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is abruptly cut off mid-equation, leaving the construction incomplete and preventing any verification of <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\notin L^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∉</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\notin L^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\neq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\neq p</annotation></semantics></math>. While the theorem statement is accurately formulated, the proof lacks the necessary calculations to show that the <math id="A15.I6.i21.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm is finite while others are infinite, rendering the response mathematically insufficient. This partial effort, demonstrating basic conceptual understanding but failing to deliver a complete proof, justifies a score of 0.5 out of 1.0.”</p>
