<p id="A15.I6.i23.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A15.I6.i23.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: grok-4-0709 (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A15.I6.i23.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: deepseek-v2-prover</span> (Score 0.5): “The student’s initial construction of <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math> fails to ensure membership in <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, as the integral <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\int_{\log 2}^{\infty}u^{-p}\,du" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_{\log 2}^{\infty}u^{-p}\,du</annotation></semantics></math> diverges for <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the requirement that <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math> regardless of <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>’s value in <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>. Although the student identifies this issue and proposes an alternative construction on <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the solution abruptly ends without computing the <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{p}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm or verifying that <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\notin L^{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∉</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>q</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\notin L^{q}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A15.I6.i23.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\neq p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\neq p</annotation></semantics></math>, leaving the proof incomplete. This demonstrates partial understanding of the need for tailored behavior near singularities but lacks the rigorous verification essential for full correctness. The measurable function should typically involve logarithmic terms adjusted to control integrability precisely at the boundary between convergence and divergence for different exponents. Overall, the attempt earns half credit for recognizing the flaw in the first construction and initiating a correction, but it falls short of a complete, accurate proof.”</p>
</div></li>
