<div id="A16.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.5): “The solution has the right overall strategy—use the exponential generating function for the Eulerian polynomials and substitute <math id="A16.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=-1</annotation></semantics></math> to produce the alternating sum <math id="A16.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k}(-1)^{k}E(n,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k}(-1)^{k}E(n,k)</annotation></semantics></math>—which merits substantial partial credit. However, the stated Eulerian EGF is not correct as written (it is missing the standard <math id="A16.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{(1-t)x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{(1-t)x}</annotation></semantics></math> factor in the numerator), so the subsequent substitution and simplification are not justified from the provided formula. Moreover, the computation is left unfinished (it even truncates mid-expression) and never connects the resulting generating function to the known EGF for alternating permutations (<math id="A16.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sec x+\tan x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>sec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sec x+\tan x</annotation></semantics></math>, up to a sign convention), so the required equality is not proved. For these reasons the work demonstrates a promising method but does not complete a correct proof, justifying a score of <math id="A16.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
