<li id="A16.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: claude-opus-4-5-20251101 (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.75): “The student correctly identifies the approach using generating functions for Eulerian numbers and appropriately substitutes t = -1 to obtain the alternating sum. The generating function identity stated is correct, and the substitution yields 2/(1+<math id="A16.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{2x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2x}</annotation></semantics></math>), which relates to the generating function for Euler numbers (secant/tangent). However, the solution is incomplete as it cuts off before finishing the derivation connecting 2/(1+<math id="A16.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{2x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{2x}</annotation></semantics></math>) to the generating function for alternating permutations, which is sec(x) + tan(x). The mathematical framework and approach are sound, but the proof lacks the final crucial steps establishing the explicit connection to <math id="A16.I1.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
