<p id="A16.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: GPT-5 Pro</span> (Score 0.25): “The submitted “solution” contains no mathematical work: it neither proves the stated floor identity nor provides any inequalities or reasoning involving <math id="A16.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. It also does not use the identity to derive a closed form for the recursion <math id="A16.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}=\lfloor\sqrt{2A_{n-1}(A_{n-1}+1)}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}=\lfloor\sqrt{2A_{n-1}(A_{n-1}+1)}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, nor does it present any formula for <math id="A16.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="A16.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A16.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. The only content is a restatement of the problem followed by an error message, so there is essentially no evidence of understanding or correctness. A small amount of credit (0.25/1.0) is appropriate only for reproducing the problem statement, with no substantive mathematical solution.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> (Score 0.5): “The solution only restates the problem and begins a setup by introducing <math id="A16.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{m}=\sqrt{2}^{m}+\sqrt{2}^{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{m}=\sqrt{2}^{m}+\sqrt{2}^{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, but it does not actually prove the key identity <math id="A16.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\sqrt{2X(X+1)}\rfloor=\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\sqrt{2X(X+1)}\rfloor=\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. No bounding argument is provided to justify the floor manipulations, and the derivation stops before any substantive inequality or conclusion is reached. Likewise, the required closed-form expression for <math id="A16.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from the recursion is not derived or even meaningfully developed. Partial credit is warranted only for initiating a relevant notation/setup, hence <math id="A16.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5/1.0</annotation></semantics></math>.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Gemini 3.0 Pro</span> (Score 0.5): “The submission does not actually establish the required floor identity: it begins by defining <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{m}=\sqrt{2}^{m}+\sqrt{2}^{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{m}=\sqrt{2}^{m}+\sqrt{2}^{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math> but stops before producing the needed inequalities comparing <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and before relating <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the key step—showing that taking <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\sqrt{2X(X+1)}\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\sqrt{2X(X+1)}\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> advances the <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j-1,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j-1,j)</annotation></semantics></math> expression to <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(j,j+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(j,j+1)</annotation></semantics></math>—is missing. The second part (deriving a closed form for <math id="A16.I3.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) is also not addressed, since it depends on the identity and an induction argument. Minimal partial credit is warranted only for a correct initial setup/notation suggesting the intended structure of the proof, but the work is far from complete.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: o3-deep-research</span> (Score 0.5): “The solution makes a reasonable start by setting <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and using <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\lfloor\alpha\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\lfloor\alpha\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> to derive <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and correctly scales this inequality by <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to relate it to <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the key step needed for the identity is to compare <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, whereas the work instead takes square roots of <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1</annotation></semantics></math>, producing bounds on <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> that are not aligned with <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof stops mid-argument and never establishes the stated floor equality. The required second part (deriving a closed form for <math id="A16.I3.i4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) is not attempted. Thus the work earns partial credit only for the correct initial inequality setup, but not for completing the proof or the recurrence analysis.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: o3-deep-research</span> (Score 0.5): “The solution correctly starts by setting <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{2}^{j-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and using <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\lfloor\alpha\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\lfloor\alpha\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the fundamental bound <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1</annotation></semantics></math>, and it also correctly scales this inequality by <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the subsequent step applies <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\cdot}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mo>⋅</mo></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\cdot}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\leq\alpha&lt;X+1</annotation></semantics></math> and produces bounds involving <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is not the quantity needed: the target expression is <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and controlling that requires working with <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X(X+1)\leq\alpha(\alpha+1)&lt;(X+1)(X+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X(X+1)\leq\alpha(\alpha+1)&lt;(X+1)(X+2)</annotation></semantics></math> or an equivalent product inequality. The proof stops before establishing the floor identity, and no closed-form for <math id="A16.I3.i5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived from the claimed relationship. Thus the work demonstrates some correct initial setup but does not complete the required argument or reach the final result, justifying partial credit (0.5/1.0).”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
