<p id="A16.I3.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.25): “The submitted solution contains only the problem restatement and the beginning of a proof, but the proof is truncated immediately (“Let <math id="A16.I3.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{\ldots}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=k(\sqrt{2}^{j}+\sqrt{\ldots}</annotation></semantics></math>”) and provides no valid inequalities or floor-function reasoning establishing the claimed identity. There is also no subsequent use of the identity to derive the closed-form expression for <math id="A16.I3.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a required second part of the problem. Because essentially no mathematically checkable argument is presented beyond minimal setup, only very limited partial credit is warranted. Hence the score of <math id="A16.I3.i6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25/1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25/1.0</annotation></semantics></math> reflects acknowledgement of an attempted start but not a correct or complete solution.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I3.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I3.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: Claude Sonnet 4.5</span> (Score 0.25): “The submission essentially restates the problem as a theorem but does not provide a complete proof of the key identity: the proof environment is cut off almost immediately and contains no valid inequalities or floor-function argument establishing the claimed equality. Because the identity is not proven, the second required part—deriving a closed-form expression for <math id="A16.I3.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> from the identity—is also entirely missing. The work therefore demonstrates only minimal progress (recognizing what must be shown and setting up a theorem/proof structure) but no substantive mathematical justification. This warrants only partial credit (0.25/1.0) for a correct setup without a completed solution.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I3.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I3.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: deepseek-v2-prover</span> (Score 0.25): “The submission only restates the problem in new notation and gives the trivial floor bound <math id="A16.I3.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\alpha+\beta)-1&lt;X\leq k(\alpha+\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\alpha+\beta)-1&lt;X\leq k(\alpha+\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, but it does not carry this through to the key comparison needed to prove <math id="A16.I3.i8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big\lfloor\sqrt{2X(X+1)}\big\rfloor=\big\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\big\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big\lfloor\sqrt{2X(X+1)}\big\rfloor=\big\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\big\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, there is no argument showing that <math id="A16.I3.i8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> lies between the consecutive integers <math id="A16.I3.i8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A16.I3.i8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\rfloor+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor k(\sqrt{2}^{j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{j})\rfloor+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is essential for a floor identity. The solution also omits the second required part entirely: using the identity to derive a closed-form expression for <math id="A16.I3.i8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Credit is therefore limited to minimal setup/notation and a basic floor inequality, with no substantive proof or final result.”</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A16.I3.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A16.I3.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A16.I3.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A16.I3.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge: gpt-5.2-pro (course-specific-rubric.txt)</span> <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A16.I3.i9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Author: deepseek-v2-prover</span> (Score 0.25): “The submission only rewrites the problem with substitutions <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\sqrt{2}^{\,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\sqrt{2}^{\,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\sqrt{2}^{\,j-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\sqrt{2}^{\,j-1}</annotation></semantics></math> and correctly notes the basic floor bound <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\alpha+\beta)-1&lt;X\leq k(\alpha+\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\alpha+\beta)-1&lt;X\leq k(\alpha+\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is minor but relevant setup. However, it never carries out the key step of comparing <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2X(X+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2X(X+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(\sqrt{2}^{\,j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{\,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(\sqrt{2}^{\,j+1}+\sqrt{2}^{\,j})</annotation></semantics></math> via sharp inequalities to justify equality after applying <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lfloor\cdot\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lfloor\cdot\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof of the stated identity is therefore incomplete, and the second required part (deriving a closed form for <math id="A16.I3.i9.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>) is not addressed at all. Hence only minimal partial credit is earned for correct preliminary notation and the trivial floor inequality.”</p>
</div></li>
